index 4479b53..f751953 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -2,6 +2,20 @@
 
 This project is a sample news application built with Astro, designed to serve as a testing ground for new features in Chrome DevTools.
 
+## Reader accounts and passkeys
+
+Account settings are available at `/devtools-times/account`. Password sign-in still validates credentials with the existing Times account service, then creates an HTTP-only, same-site session. Settings support passkey registration, listing, nicknames, removal, and reader display-name updates. Registered passkeys can also be used on the sign-in page. Removing the last key leaves password sign-in available.
+
+Use Node 22.13 or later. Account records, verified credential public keys, and one-use challenges are stored in SQLite at `.data/accounts.sqlite`; sessions live in `.data/sessions`. No passwords or private keys are stored. Mount `.data` on durable storage for deployment. `ACCOUNT_DB_PATH` can override the SQLite file path at runtime. This storage setup supports one application instance (or workers sharing the same local disk); deployments across multiple machines need a shared database and session driver.
+
+The default WebAuthn origin is `https://astro-news-pytrpecapa-uc.a.run.app`, matching `astro.config.mjs`. For other deployments set `WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN` to the exact public origin (scheme, host, and optional port; no path or trailing slash), and optionally `WEBAUTHN_RP_ID` to the registration domain. Its default is the hostname of that configured origin. For local work, configure `WEBAUTHN_ORIGIN=http://localhost:11224` and `WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=localhost`. Production requires HTTPS. Existing passkeys stay bound to the RP ID used to register them.
+
+The browser receives the server's RP ID, Base64URL account ID, and complete accepted-key list. Supported browsers synchronize accepted keys on settings load and immediately after removal; account display-name changes synchronize user details. A passkey nickname is only a site-side label. Unsupported or declining authenticators leave server-side changes intact, with guidance to manage saved entries in the password manager. Malformed IDs skip the entire sync so a partial list cannot inadvertently hide valid keys.
+
+Provider hints use a small local snapshot from the [passkey-authenticator-aaguids registry](https://github.com/passkeydeveloper/passkey-authenticator-aaguids), captured November 13, 2026. This registry is being retired in favor of FIDO MDS; no runtime fetch depends on its availability. Unknown or zero AAGUIDs show a generic key icon. Provider metadata is never used for authorization.
+
+Run `pnpm test` for isolated API, cryptographic registration/sign-in, ownership, replay, and synchronization tests. They use a temporary database and a software authenticator, without starting the application server. Run `pnpm build` for the production build.
+
 
 ```sh
 pnpm install && pnpm run start
